From: HardDinD developers
Subject: um: support x86-64 rseq and interrupt-driven IRQ work

Wire x86-64 UML into the generic rseq signal and syscall hooks. The normal
notify-resume path supplies CPU IDs and handles migration fixups. Retain a
Kconfig opt-in for other UML configurations.

RSEQ enables SCHED_MM_CID, whose last-user and exec cleanup invokes
irq_work_sync(). Without an architecture interrupt, that routine always
waits for an RCU grace period, even for an unused work item. Two such waits
turn fork+exec into roughly 85 ms on UML, regardless of rseq registration.

Use a coalescible IPI to drain the generic raised/lazy lists in hardirq
context. The existing host signal and deferred-IPI machinery preserves IRQ
masking and wakes idle CPUs. Keep the tick fallback until the boot CPU's
IPI transport is initialized, then explicitly drain early queued work.
harddind_irq_work=0 retains the old path for A/B comparisons.
UP builds retain the generic fallback. No RCU wait is bypassed: the generic
IRQ-work implementation selects its interrupt-safe synchronization path.

--- a/arch/um/Kconfig
+++ b/arch/um/Kconfig
@@ -46,6 +46,17 @@
 	select SPARSE_IRQ
 	select MMU_GATHER_RCU_TABLE_FREE
 
+config UML_X86_RSEQ
+	bool "Experimental x86-64 restartable sequences"
+	depends on X86_64
+	select HAVE_RSEQ
+	default n
+	help
+	  Enable the generic rseq ABI for x86-64 UML tasks. CPU IDs come
+	  from the guest scheduler; scheduling and signal return use the
+	  generic rseq fixups. This experimental option is off by default
+	  pending migration, signal, fork/exec and workload validation.
+
 config MMU
 	bool
 	default y
